/*****************************************************************************
    jitXtemplate -- THE PROPOSED genKantParse SEQUENCE TEMPLATE, JITTED.
    2026-08-08. The recommendation, measured before it is made.

    THE SKETCH'S SPELLING IS NOT AVAILABLE. Measured today:
        `a AND b`   exit 139, and NO degrade line -- it crashes before the
                    counter sees it (incant/jitXand2)
        `a OR b`    exit 0, degrade 0, SILENTLY WRONG -- fire 2 wants 1 and
                    gets 0, the emit-time value folded (incant/jitXor)
        `!field`    inert on a field CARRYING a value, both engines agreeing,
                    so it is a language question and not a JIT one
                    (incant/jitXnot)

    THIS IS THE REPLACEMENT, BUILT ONLY FROM CERTIFIED CONSTRUCTS: comparison
    (gated, ladder J2/J3), if (gated, jitEmitGIF, both arms), mid-block return
    (gated, ladder JRt), and a value-returning callee consumed sequentially
    (measured green today at incant/jitXseq, degrade 2 for E2 tail returns).

        xtRule code={
            xtSuk = xtT1();
            if xtSuk == 0;   return 0;
            xtSuk = xtT2();
            if xtSuk == 0;   return 0;
            return 1;
            };

    IT SHORT-CIRCUITS BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by an operator that would
    have to decline to evaluate. That is the point: a parse term consumes
    input, so a right arm that runs after a failed left arm advances the mark
    past text the rule never matched. The AND spelling cannot express that
    today in EITHER engine (CLAIM KANT-34, and its reason is structural); this
    spelling does not need to.

    xtTicks IS THE SHORT-CIRCUIT PROOF AND IT IS A COUNT (H4):
        fire 1  t1 fails  ->  ticks 1   t2 NEVER RAN
        fire 2  both pass ->  ticks 3   (1 from fire 1, plus 2)
    A ticks of 2 on fire 1 would mean t2 ran anyway, which is the eager
    behaviour this template exists to avoid.

    xtOut carries the rule's verdict, and the two fires must differ.

    ORDER (bear-trap #25): jitted half FIRST, oracle last. Sentinel (H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitXtemplate);
define
    xtOK1;
    xtOK2;
    xtTicks;
    xtSuk;
    xtOut;
    xtT1 code={
        xtTicks = xtTicks + 1;
        return xtOK1;
        };
    xtT2 code={
        xtTicks = xtTicks + 1;
        return xtOK2;
        };
    xtRule code={
        xtSuk = xtT1();
        if xtSuk == 0;
            return 0;
        xtSuk = xtT2();
        if xtSuk == 0;
            return 0;
        return 1;
        };
    ;

xtOK1   = 0;
xtOK2   = 1;
xtTicks = 0;
xtOut   = 9;
print "XT fire 1 setup : xtOK1 =" xtOK1 " (first term FAILS)":;
testing(xtRule);
print "XT fire 1 result: ticks =" xtTicks " (want 1 -- 2 means no short-circuit)":;

xtOK1 = 1;
xtOK2 = 1;
print "XT fire 2 setup : xtOK1 =" xtOK1 " xtOK2 =" xtOK2 " (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "XT fire 2 result: ticks =" xtTicks " (want 3 -- both terms ran)":;

xtOK1   = 0;
xtOK2   = 1;
xtTicks = 0;
xtRule();
print "XT interpreted  : ticks =" xtTicks " (the oracle -- want 1)":;

print "XT SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
